{
  "q0": "PhD B noted that the major component in the second waveform was actually the air conditioner, which introduced a significant noise at 5 Hz. He observed that this noise was not a good illustration of room reverberation but rather of room noise, and pointed out that it was inappropriate as a demonstration because it wasn't reverberation but a known noise source. He also mentioned that the visual spectrogram differences, particularly in high frequencies, were noticeable and that the noise attenuated the high-frequency components in distant recordings.",
  "q1": "The discussion on Eurospeech covered the upcoming deadline in Denmark (Aalborg) on May 13th, with a special session on Aurora having an extended deadline. The group was working on experiments with the Meeting Recorder digits and considered integrating spectral subtraction from Ericsson. There was also a discussion about using a second stream for fine structure analysis, and the possibility of using a tandem system with a voiced-unvoiced detector. The group acknowledged that while spectral subtraction could improve speech-to-noise ratios, it may not work well with on-line normalization due to increased variance in the signal, and further experiments were needed to evaluate its effectiveness.",
  "q2": "The discussion on patterns of noise in the recordings highlighted that the major noise component in the second waveform was the air conditioner, specifically at 5 Hz. This noise was not a good representation of room reverberation but rather of room noise. The group recognized that this noise significantly affected the signal, particularly in distant recordings, and that the high-frequency components were attenuated. They also discussed that the visual spectrogram clearly showed spectral effects, with the distant recording having flatter spectra, and that the noise was not a result of reverberation but of a known external noise source.",
  "q3": "PhD F thought that Eurospeech was a key opportunity for the group to present their work, especially with the extended deadline for the Aurora special session. He noted that the group was working on integrating spectral subtraction from Ericsson and that they were also developing their own spectral subtraction method. He mentioned that while the results from spectral subtraction were initially poor, they improved after applying mean and variance normalization, and that the integration of such techniques with on-line normalization was still under investigation due to potential issues with increased variance and musical noise."
}

