{
  "q0": "The deployed model shows mixed performance: out of six matching conditions, three cases improved, one worsened, and two stayed about the same. On average, the model achieved improvement of around 49% in error reduction across tasks, with Danish showing better results than German. Despite some bugs, the overall performance is considered good, with error rates reduced by a third to half depending on the case. However, the best results (e.g., 54% reduction) are still below usability thresholds, especially in the well-matched Danish case where error rates reach 8–9% per digit, making it non-viable for real-world use.",
  "q1": "PhD C asked about experiments using data from multiple languages to train neural nets. The professor noted that such experiments were tried but did not work well, possibly due to label inconsistencies. The team found that hand-labeled data performed best, and obtaining hand-labeled data from other languages is difficult. The discussion concluded that while it would be interesting to explore, it may not be feasible with current resources.",
  "q2": "The professor viewed the project as having significant practical value due to its use of realistic, real-world acoustic conditions (e.g., car environments with real noise and reverberation) rather than artificially simulated ones. This realism makes the results more applicable to real deployments, even though the performance is still poor. The professor emphasized that the project's failure to achieve usable results highlights the difficulty of real-world speech recognition and underscores the need for deeper investigation into data and feature processing.",
  "q3": "The discussion on the pragmatic overview emphasized that the project uses realistic, real-world speech data (e.g., from cars with real noise and reverberation) rather than artificial simulations, which increases its practical relevance. Despite improvements (30–60% error reduction), performance remains poor—especially in real-world conditions—making the system unusable for practical applications. The professor highlighted that this realism is valuable because it reflects actual deployment scenarios, and the poor results indicate significant challenges in handling real-world acoustic variability, necessitating further research into data, features, and system design."
}

